{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3157","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3157","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3157\",\"parent_revision\":\"77e964a22e72850684596f9a105c1f7509aa56809c06dd7abae34bdbd0def1cc\",\"root_revision\":\"77e964a22e72850684596f9a105c1f7509aa56809c06dd7abae34bdbd0def1cc\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P0\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-22\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThree grooming-agent writes to #3152 returned success and persisted nothing usable. The agents' work was lost each time with no error surfaced to the caller.\\n\\nEvent 1 \u2014 impact-analyst wrote the Impact Radius section. `backlog_groom` returned `{\\\"groomed_updated\\\": true}`. The section came back holding only its scope-summary paragraph; the six required categories, Systems Inventory, and Ecosystem Completeness Checklist were absent.\\n\\nEvent 2 \u2014 fact-checker wrote the Fact-Check section. Same success return. The section came back containing exactly one entry whose entire content was the fragment `<div><sub>2026-08-22T14:58:39.771586Z</sub>` \u2014 no verdicts at all.\\n\\nEvent 3 \u2014 fact-checker retried with `replace_section` plus a reason. Same success return. The section came back containing only `<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\\\ n\\\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-08-22T15:08:09.685995Z \u2014 Replace corrupted entry with complete fact-check verdicts</summary>`. Nine claims of verification work lost a second time.\\n\\nTwo contributing causes were reproduced directly and are fixed on branch `fix/3152-no-generated-story-acceptance-criteria` (commit `957194655`): `wrap_entry` double-wrapping already-wrapped content, and `_parse_entry_timestamp` raising `ValueError: year 0 is out of range` on the zero-date fallback. Neither of those accounts for what remains here.\\n\\nWhat remains, in two parts.\\n\\nPart 1 \u2014 the write path reports success when it has discarded content. Every one of the three calls returned a success payload. Nothing in the return value distinguished \\\"wrote your content\\\" from \\\"wrote a truncated fragment of your content\\\" from \\\"wrote none of your content\\\". A caller \u2014 human or agent \u2014 has no way to detect the loss except by reading the section back and comparing, which nothing instructs it to do. `.claude/rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` states that a function performing side effects must return a value indicating what changed, and that callers must use it. This path violates that rule at its most consequential point. Establishing whether the write itself failed, or whether the write succeeded and a later read/round-trip destroyed the content, is part of this work \u2014 the observations above cannot yet distinguish those.\\n\\nPart 2 \u2014 a section whose entry tags are unbalanced absorbs the following section. Reproduced against current source:\\n\\n    corrupt = '<div><sub>TS</sub>\\\\n\\\\n<div><sub>inner</sub>\\\\n\\\\nold\\\\n</div>'\\n    out = rewrite_section(corrupt, new_content='NEW', replace=True, reason='r', added_date='2026-08-22')\\n    full = '## Fact-Check\\\\n\\\\n' + out + '\\\\n\\\\n## Context\\\\n\\\\nauthored.\\\\n'\\n    parse_md_body_sections(full, added_date='0000-00-00')\\n\\nThe Fact-Check section parses to three entries. The third has an empty string as its id and its content is `## Context\\\\n\\\\nauthored.` \u2014 the entire next section, pulled into the previous one. `ENTRY_RE` (`backlog_core/entry_blocks.py:11`) is `<div><sub>([^<]+)</sub>\\\\s*(.*?)</div>` with `re.DOTALL`; it has no notion of balanced tags or of section boundaries, so once a stray `</div>` desynchronises it the parser reads straight through the next `##` heading.\\n\\nAn entry with an empty id is structurally impossible for authored content \u2014 every real entry carries a timestamp. Its presence is a reliable corruption signal that nothing currently checks. `_parse_entry_timestamp(\\\"\\\")` raises, so any `since=` filter over an affected section also fails.\\n\\nConsequence worth stating plainly: content loss here is not confined to the section being written. A single malformed write can silently consume an adjacent authored section.\\n\\nWhy P0 rather than P1: this is the storage layer for every backlog item, every grooming output, and every artifact the SAM pipeline depends on. It destroys authored work, reports success while doing so, and the corruption propagates across section boundaries. The three events above happened inside a single grooming run of one item.\\n\\nThe remediation is a parser change rather than a local patch, which is why it is filed rather than fixed inline: `ENTRY_RE` needs boundary awareness (or section splitting must complete before entry parsing, with the two never able to disagree), and the write path needs to report what it actually persisted. Both are design decisions with blast radius across `entry_blocks.py`, `parsing.py`, and `operations.py`.\\n\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **backlog_groom reports success while discarding the content it was given, and a section with unbalanced entry tags absorbs the next section** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 2026-08-23, three live content-loss events while grooming #3152\\n- **Priority**: P0\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-22T15:31:08.043432Z</sub>\\n\\nCORRECTION 2026-08-23 \u2014 the root cause stated in this item's description is wrong, and its Part 2 reproduction does not demonstrate the observed failure.\\n\\nWhat was actually observed on #3152. The item now carries 46 top-level sections. Sections named \\\"Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\\", \\\"Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\\", \\\"Documentation (will become stale)\\\", \\\"Systems Inventory\\\" and \\\"Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\\" are the impact-analyst agent's level-3 subheadings promoted to top level. Roughly 28 sections named \\\"Claim A:\\\" through \\\"Claim I:\\\" are the fact-checker's subheadings, repeated across its three write attempts. The damage is section explosion, not truncation: content was split on its own markdown headings, and the fragment that appeared to survive in each section was simply the text preceding the first level-3 heading.\\n\\nWhy the original description got it wrong. The reproduction in Part 2 was constructed to match the hypothesis \u2014 nested entry wrappers with no headings \u2014 instead of using the agents' actual submitted content, which contained both nesting and level-3 headings. It reproduced a real but different defect and proved nothing about the observed failure.\\n\\nWhat cannot be reproduced against current source. No combination tried produces the explosion: wrapped entry content containing level-3 headings through extract_sections; the same with level-2 headings; pre-wrapped nested content containing level-3 headings; and the render_issue_body to parse_issue_body round trip with a level-3 heading inside an entry. The _EntryDivBlock guard inside _split_body_h2 holds in every case. That guard landed in commit bb8ee0c0a, \\\"unify section-key normalization between local writes and GitHub-parsed sections (#2964)\\\", dated 2026-08-18, and it is on main.\\n\\nLeading hypothesis, not established. extract_sections' own docstring records that the marko-AST splitter replaced a naive line-based scan which misidentified heading-shaped lines inside entry-block content as section boundaries \u2014 precisely the observed behaviour. The live failures came through the MCP server, which runs a cached build rather than working-tree source. So the explosion may be a stale-cache artifact of code predating bb8ee0c0a. This is under investigation. It is not yet confirmed, and the running server's actual revision has not been determined; the plugin cache was not found under the expected path.\\n\\nWhat in this item survives the correction. Part 1 stands unchanged and is independent of the cause of the explosion: every one of the three destructive writes returned groomed_updated true, and no part of the return value let a caller distinguish persisting the given content from persisting a fragment of it from persisting none of it. That violates the repo's silent-failure-prevention rule at its most consequential point and is worth fixing regardless of what caused the loss.\\n\\nPart 2 must be re-scoped. If the parser boundary defect is not reachable in current source, this item's remaining scope is the write-path reporting contract plus, separately, cleaning the 40-plus junk sections now sitting on #3152.\\n\\nTwo defects found while investigating this are already fixed on branch fix/3152-no-generated-story-acceptance-criteria, commit 957194655, and are in PR #3160. Neither caused the observed explosion. First, wrap_entry double-wrapped content that already carried an entry wrapper, producing a leaked wrapper inside entry content and an orphan entry with an empty id. Second, the documented since filter raised ValueError \\\"year 0 is out of range\\\" on any section whose entries fell back to the zero-date id, making that filter unusable on legacy unwrapped content.\\n\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"d3a5209d91fd6606515bdf2eb6c147c4262cf022f110d70758bc45d130fc1fb2\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQL44vg\"}"}